failures teach you."

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.610
You have to learn from them.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:21.270
Which is what Chaos is
really about, right?

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.840
Our systems break all the time.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.130
There's a researcher on
safety named David Woods.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.570
He gets mentioned in a lot
dev ops sort of circles.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.320
And he has this quote
that our systems are

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.300
in a constant state of failure.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.130
Failure isn't binary.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.360
It's just a spectrum.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.560
And so if we understand
that, that we're failing,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.200
and that's OK, right?

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.173
Because we can learn from it.

00:01:45.173 --> 00:01:47.340
Now Barack Obama talked
about a whole lot of things.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.290
He talked a lot about hope.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.150
Unfortunately, that's
maybe not a good thing.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.090
So I love this quote about hope.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.810
Corey Bertram is the VP
of Infrastructure and SRE

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.000
at Datadog.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.590
And he likes to say this,
that hope is not a strategy.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.590
We can't hope that our
systems will stay up.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.720
We can't hope that our software
will deploy without any bugs.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.900
How many people deployed
sometime this week?

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.790
Deployed some code,
deployed infrastructure,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:15.540
spun something up?

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.650
That's most of us.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.140
How many people
deployed bad code?

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.990
You had a bug, or you deployed
infrastructure and it broke?

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.870
And that's pretty much
the same amount of hands.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.680
If you didn't,
then I don't know--

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.490
good job on you?

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:30.840
Or maybe you missed something.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.215
And you should be
really worried.

00:02:34.593 --> 00:02:36.260
So the other thing
about Corey, though--

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.718
and the reason that
Corey says this

00:02:37.718 --> 00:02:39.120
is that Corey came from Netflix.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.530
So he was part of the team
that started implementing

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.360
a lot of chaos around things.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.700
And so the story of this is that
Netflix was moving from on prem

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.270
to the cloud.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.630
And as they were doing so,
they realized a lot of things

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:54.950
about the cloud.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.050
They realized that cloud
isn't about resiliency.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.400
Even though Google
provides these great SLAs

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.470
and they're up most of the time,
just like us, there are issues.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.007
And they work to resolve them.

00:03:08.007 --> 00:03:09.590
But the great thing
about Google Cloud

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:13.610
is that you can spin up
new resources all the time.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:18.570
So you have this ability to
constantly have more resources.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.540
And that means that rather
than avoiding failure,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.710
you can embrace failure, you
can start planning for failure

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:28.190
and doing things like
rollovers, or auto scaling,

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:29.640
and things like that.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.510
So they had a lot
of these lessons.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.980
The number one lesson that
I like from this blog post

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.040
that they posted was that
the best way to avoid failure

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.550
is to constantly fail, to
constantly try to fail.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:42.350
Do those painful things.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.420
And then learn from them.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.910
And this led them to creating
a thing called Chaos Monkeys.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.603
Who here has heard
of Chaos Monkey?

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:49.470
Yeah.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.060
I think we all
have at this point.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.040
Who here has heard that
it's this program that

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.520
randomly deletes your
servers, takes things down?

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.150
Yeah.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.070
I mean, that was
the point, right?

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.100
Unfortunately, I think a lot
of us have believed this.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.390
And if you go back and
read the blog post,

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:05.610
that's sort of a myth.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.500
And I'll dive into
that in a bit.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.140
So who am I?

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.590
I don't come from Netflix.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:11.520
I'm Jason.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.840
I work at Datadog.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.840
I'm a technical evangelist,
which is a really fun job.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.600
It means I get to come and
talk to people like you.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:19.920
I get to help our customers.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.670
I get to learn stuff, and
then teach people, as well.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.770
A bunch of things about me--

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.510
I do actually have
a hat like that.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:28.920
I play a lot of Pokemon Go.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.110
If you see me wandering
around later, I'm not lost.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.420
A little bit about Datadog.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.330
Datadog is a SaaS-based
monitoring platform.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.620
So we work with Google.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.285
We work with your on prem.

00:04:41.285 --> 00:04:43.160
If you're doing multi-cloud,
we work with all

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.150
the major cloud providers.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.870
But we ingest all of your
metrics, your traces,

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.520
distributed traces.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.220
We collect your logs,
help you do log analytics.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.620
And we launched a new
synthetics product.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.310
So essentially, we try to get
you all the observability tools

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.532
to let you know what's
going on in your systems,

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.990
let you know where you can find
those issues when something

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:03.920
does happen.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.050
And this is important
when it comes to Chaos.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.825
So back to it to Netflix and
talking about Chaos, right?

00:05:11.825 --> 00:05:14.200
If you actually go back to
their blog post, it says this.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.060
That by running Chaos Monkey--

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.580
not randomly, not
at any given time,

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.180
but in the middle
of a business day

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.960
in a carefully monitored
environment with engineers

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.190
standing by--

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.410
well, then we can
address those problems

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.050
and learn the
lessons that we need

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.480
to learn about the
weaknesses in our systems,

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.490
about how to build the
automatic recovery mechanisms--

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.150
which is one thing, as
mentioned, that the cloud now

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.100
enables us to do.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.940
And so by doing all of this,
then maybe at 3 AM on a Sunday,

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:48.520
on those random times, then
we won't even notice, right?

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.280
We can build all these things.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.620
So lesson one from this
talk, if you come out of here

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:54.995
and you're, like,
yeah, I'm going

00:05:54.995 --> 00:05:56.080
to go do some Chaos stuff.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.710
And you're doing it in
the middle of the night.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.080
And you're not telling
your colleagues.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:00.950
And you're waking them up
in the middle of the night,

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:01.900
then you're a jerk.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.172
And my number one lesson
is don't be a jerk.

00:06:06.172 --> 00:06:07.630
We'll talk about
how to plan these.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.790
We'll have talked
about how to run these

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:14.500
so that you're actually learning
and being a good colleague.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.385
So at Datadog, we
start with Game Days.

00:06:16.385 --> 00:06:18.510
And the main difference
between Game Days and Chaos

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.610
is that Game Days are very
much a manual process.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.580
And that's where you
want to start, right?

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.890
You don't want to just
jump into automation

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.050
that you don't understand.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.250
So just like any game at
Datadog, there are rules.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:33.270
I was in Italy.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.538
So this is the
Italian soccer team.

00:06:35.538 --> 00:06:37.830
And I was pointing out to
them that the rules are there

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.690
to make the game fun.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.860
If you were playing
a soccer game,

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.590
and there were no
rules, well, then

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.260
I can come up and punch you
in the face and take the ball

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.750
and run, throw it in the goal.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:49.500
And you'd be like,
hey, that's not soccer.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.280
That sounds like rugby.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.720
It's only fun if there are
rules that we all agree upon.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.230
And so the rules make
things a little less scary.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.330
They let us know what to expect.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.200
So the rules of the Game Day
are just like a soccer match.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.970
It lasts 90 minutes.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:07.140
We want to time box this.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.515
We don't want it to go on for
a full day, because then we

00:07:09.515 --> 00:07:10.770
lose productivity.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.950
We don't want things
to just drag on and on,

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.690
so we keep things
nice and short.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.713
Within that 90 minutes, we
spend 30 minutes for planning.

00:07:18.713 --> 00:07:20.130
Again, we want to
keep this short.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:24.030
Nobody wants to spend half
a day or a day planning what

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.210
they're going to do--

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:29.590
so 30 minutes, nice short
period, doesn't take too long.

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.720
Then we spend 50
minutes playing.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:32.890
This is the actual fun part.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.670
This is where we get
to test our systems.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:38.790
This is where we get to break
things and see what happens.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.990
And then again, 10 minutes at
the end just for reporting.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:43.730
And we don't want
this to be too long.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:45.530
Again, if it's too
long for reporting,

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.520
nobody wants to do it, right?

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.110
That's the boring
part of things.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.410
So the 10 minutes, nice
and short, and I'll

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:54.510
dive into to how
this works in a bit.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:55.970
But let's talk about the team.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.050
Every game has teams.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:07:59.690
So our teams are nice and small.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.850
We don't want to take
up everybody's time,

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:04.170
because again, we
lose productivity.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:07.350
So the team ends up being
two, usually two people.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:08.990
The first is a
subject matter expert.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:11.060
And that's going to
be the developer who

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.360
built the service, who
knows the most about it.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.040
It might be the ops
engineer who architected

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:18.710
this particular
service's infrastructure.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.420
Whoever knows the
most about it, that's

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:25.580
the person that's going to be
the main player on this team.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.878
The next person is going to
be someone from the SRE team.

00:08:28.878 --> 00:08:30.170
So they're going to partner up.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.179
Because the SREs are
really those experts

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:34.140
on how to keep your
systems running,

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.580
how to keep them reliable,
what the best practices are.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.679
So now we can partner
up with the person who's

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:42.200
building things, the person
who essentially helps

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.159
ensure that they stay running.

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:45.870
It's a good team.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.940
Sometimes, though, we
add a third player.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.190
And that third player is
going to be a junior engineer

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.330
or a junior developer.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:55.360
And more often than not, I like
when we have a third player.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:58.300
Because that third player, this
junior engineer, is the person

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:01.090
that knows just a little
bit about that service,

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:03.710
or just a little bit
about that infrastructure.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:07.510
So when you take something down
and you see some weird symptoms

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.460
around that, this
is the person that

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:11.710
has no idea why that happened.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:14.770
They're the person that asks
those questions, because there

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:15.790
are no stupid questions.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.748
But they ask those
questions about why did that

00:09:17.748 --> 00:09:19.623
happen, right?

00:09:19.623 --> 00:09:20.290
What's going on?

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:20.460
Wait.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:21.752
Why did we do that in the code?

00:09:21.752 --> 00:09:24.480
Why did we build that this way?

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.360
And for most of us
that have been around,

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.370
well, you're like, well,
of course, oh, yeah.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.200
That's not really
the logical way.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:33.182
We did it because there
is this other constraint.

00:09:33.182 --> 00:09:34.640
So that's the great
thing about it.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.760
The other nice thing is,
as a more senior engineer

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.430
or developer, you get
to mentor that person.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:40.940
Right?

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:43.430
Now they're seeing
the reasons that you

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:46.850
built that service or that
system in a particular way.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:48.680
They understand
now when you say,

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:51.930
oh, yeah, I did that weird
thing because this constraint.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.020
Now, as a junior engineer,
when they come up

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.970
with strange
constraints or things

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.160
that they're not
familiar with, now they

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.530
have some reference
of, like, oh, here's

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.090
a creative way that you did it.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:06.580
So let's talk about
those times, though.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:09.410
30 minutes spent planning-- what
do we do for that 30 minutes?

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.500
Well, number one, we
have to schedule this.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.660
We want to ensure that
we have enough time

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:15.350
to do this 90 minutes.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:18.610
Do not start this at
4:00 PM on a Friday.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:21.550
Because if it runs long,
if something happens,

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:23.410
it's eating into your weekend.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:24.670
So plan it.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.560
Schedule it for at
least those 90 minutes.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.300
But maybe add a
little bit of time

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:32.170
in case you need
that extra time.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:34.240
Then, simply, you
pick your tests.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:35.692
You start easy.

00:10:35.692 --> 00:10:37.150
Figure out what
you're going to do.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:38.980
Figure out what
you want to break,

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:41.455
how you want to break it.

00:10:41.455 --> 00:10:43.160
It's not too hard.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:44.660
Write down what you
expect to happen

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:46.520
when you do break things.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.460
So think of it as a
scientific experiment.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:51.710
What's your hypothesis?

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:56.090
If I take down the data store,
what's my service going to do?

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.840
What sort of alerting
should I receive?

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.640
Your hypothesis
does not have to be,

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.890
and probably shouldn't
be every single time,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.050
that your service survives.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:08.760
We'll talk about some of
the tests that we run.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:12.120
But it's really not
logical to assume

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.550
that you can build
services that,

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.940
if you cut off all their
critical dependencies,

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:18.690
if you drop the network,
that they're just

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.850
going to keep going hunky dory.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.140
Everything's fine, right?

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.530
So write down what
you expect to happen.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.270
Also write down
what your plan is.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:32.010
What's your fallback plan if
everything goes wrong, if you

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.790
have a cascading failure?

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:35.580
Let's say you start
off in staging.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.740
And you're, like, cool.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.600
I've got these
tests, no problem.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:40.900
I don't expect things to fail.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.108
So you don't write up a plan.

00:11:42.108 --> 00:11:44.250
And suddenly you take
down all of staging.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:45.810
Your entire
organization is down.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:46.980
Nobody can test the code.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:48.540
No one can work.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.890
Well, that's a bad problem.

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.980
So whatever you do
write down your Plan B.

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:56.450
What are you going to
do if things go wrong?

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:58.010
And then finally,
most importantly,

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:59.320
share the document.

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:03.400
Not just to your team, not
just to the two people playing,

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:06.430
that other SRE that's with
you, or if you're the SRE,

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:09.850
not just to the engineer
or the junior engineer.

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.950
Send it as wide as
you can, at least

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.260
to your entire engineering
organization, all

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:17.080
of your developers, all
of your ops engineers.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.450
But even consider
more broadly, right?

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:23.860
If you're starting to do
game days in production,

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:25.390
send it company wide.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:25.960
Right?

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:29.238
Think about your account
managers or your sales reps.

00:12:29.238 --> 00:12:31.780
If they're out there talking to
a customer and the customer--

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:35.770
you know, your Chaos engineering
plan kind of fell apart

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:38.860
and starts to impact latency
on your production systems,

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:40.660
and your customers
start complaining,

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:42.300
they're going to
not complain to you.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.050
They're going to
complain to their account

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:46.120
reps or the salespeople.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.860
So it's good to share
this information

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:52.010
as broadly as possible, because
it may impact other people.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.570
And it's good for them to know.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:57.890
So that's the 30 minutes,
nice short planning, not too

00:12:57.890 --> 00:12:59.840
much involved.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:00.970
So what happens during?

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:03.220
Well, during, when we
actually run the game days,

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.260
we do start off in staging.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:08.090
You want to start off
in a safe environment.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.570
And then once it passes
there, we'll promote that

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:13.990
and we'll try it in production,
during off peak hours.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.910
And then we'll move
that to prime time.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:19.960
We'll run it in production in
heavy times of actual customer

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:20.460
traffic.

00:13:23.172 --> 00:13:24.630
So when we start
the game day, when

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:27.083
we start the actual playing,
you want to announce that.

00:13:27.083 --> 00:13:29.500
And you want to announce that
within your group chat tool,

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:32.100
whatever you're
using, Hipchat, Slack,

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.110
whatever sort of chat
ops tool you're using.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:38.140
Because it's a way to get
things broadly, right?

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:40.370
If I just come into the
room and say, hey everyone,

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:41.710
I'm doing this.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:44.200
Well, you know, Bob might
be out getting coffee.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.870
He has no idea
that that happened.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:48.550
So we want to
announce it in a tool

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:51.700
that everybody has access to and
in a tool that records things.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.520
Because we want to maintain
our discussions there.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:57.070
Because if it's me and an
SRE, and we're teaming up,

00:13:57.070 --> 00:13:58.930
and we take down a system.

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:00.640
And I'm trying to type up notes.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:02.500
And things are going on.

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.262
Well, what did I say
five minutes ago?

00:14:04.262 --> 00:14:06.220
I was talking about this
thing that I observed,

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:08.670
and then you saw
this other thing.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.997
If it's all in Slack,
if it's all in Hipchat

00:14:10.997 --> 00:14:13.330
or whatever you're using, now
I have a complete dialogue

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.190
of everything that was said.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:16.750
When it comes to
taking notes later,

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:18.940
then I can just post
a link and say here's

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.060
what we saw here's
what we're discussing.

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.730
Keep things nice and easy.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.340
We also want to
monitor for outages.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.570
And we do this in two ways.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:30.150
Obviously, at Datadog,
we're a monitoring company.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.440
So we have tons of dashboards.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:33.180
You want to keep
whatever monitoring

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:36.220
tool you've got open, be
monitoring your systems.

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:40.140
But also, we have an outage
channel within Slack.

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:41.910
So we want to keep
aware of that.

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:44.940
Because if anyone
declares an actual outage,

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:47.820
that's a cue for us to
stop what we're doing.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.790
We don't want to add
our Game Day or Chaos

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:52.770
into their actual real
chaos while they're

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:56.200
trying to debug their systems
or bring systems back online.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:59.420
So you want to monitor for that.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:02.300
And then, finally, you play.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.260
You break your systems.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:04.052
You run your tests.

00:15:04.052 --> 00:15:05.210
You take your notes.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:11.200
You explore your systems,
see what they do.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.630
For the after, again, we want
to keep this nice and short.

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:15.000
Because this is the boring part.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.260
This is not the fun
part of playing.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:19.570
But the great thing
is you've taken notes.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:22.000
You have that
dialogue within Slack.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.710
So you just paste
the link to that.

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:25.120
You create cards and tickets.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:27.290
Whatever tracking
system you're using,

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.280
whether that's Jira or
Trello, whatever you've got,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:31.690
you write those down.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:33.390
The things that you
saw, like, oh, yeah,

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.497
when we broke this thing
our monitor didn't go off

00:15:35.497 --> 00:15:36.580
the way we thought it did.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.790
Or I got an alert, but it had
useless information in it.

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:42.490
So you just create the
tickets right there.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:45.318
Make sure that those get done.

00:15:45.318 --> 00:15:47.110
You write your summary
and the key lessons.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.440
And the summary is
essentially your notes.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.687
Again, you don't have to
spend a whole lot of time,

00:15:51.687 --> 00:15:53.770
you shouldn't spend a whole
lot of time with this.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:54.812
Copy your notes in there.

00:15:54.812 --> 00:15:56.593
Copy the link to Slack.

00:15:56.593 --> 00:15:58.510
Write up some of the
things that you observed,

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:00.343
those key lessons, those
key points that you

00:16:00.343 --> 00:16:02.150
want people to take away.

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:05.140
And then you email that
out to all of engineering,

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:07.210
at least, at a
very minimum, maybe

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:08.922
to your entire organization.

00:16:08.922 --> 00:16:10.630
Because again, the
whole point about this

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:12.340
is learning from these failures.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.980
So spreading that
information as far as we can

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:18.770
becomes beneficial.

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:22.260
And finally, the most important
part is you celebrate.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:25.900
At Datadog, we love to celebrate
anything and everything.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:31.690
Any possibility to celebrate,
we do, including Game Days.

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:35.140
If the Game Day goes
great, and you're, like,

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:38.480
we expected that our system
would be completely resilient.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.300
We hammered it with traffic.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.160
We essentially DDoSed
ourself and it auto

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:45.230
scaled up and handled
all that perfectly,

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:47.203
or our [INAUDIBLE]
actually blocked that,

00:16:47.203 --> 00:16:49.370
because that's what we were
expecting-- like, great.

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:49.970
You won.

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:52.310
You built a resilient system.

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:52.940
Congrats.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:54.410
You celebrate that.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:58.280
But similarly, if I roll
out my Chaos experiment,

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:00.050
and there is a
cascading failure,

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:02.480
and I take down all of staging,
and the entire engineering

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.599
department's mad at me because
everyone has to stop work,

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:07.339
well, I fix that.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:08.430
But then I celebrate.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:08.930
Right?

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:11.000
Because I just learned
something that's

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:12.589
really important
about our systems.

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:15.349
I learned where that
critical fault was.

00:17:15.349 --> 00:17:18.119
And now we're on the
road to fixing that.

00:17:18.119 --> 00:17:22.710
So you want to celebrate
everything when you do these.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:24.460
So let's talk about
the tests that we run.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:27.520
We have five different
levels, or six,

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:29.320
because we number with zero.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:32.560
But the first is to
terminate the service.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:35.680
If I turn off this service
and I just restart it,

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:37.780
does it actually restart?

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:40.960
Does it restart without
any human intervention?

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:44.500
If I have to restart my server
and then log in and enter

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:49.480
some credentials because I'm not
using a secrets manager, well,

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:50.680
that's a bad thing.

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:54.880
If I restart my application,
and because I did that,

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:58.540
because it was down, my router
isn't actually sending traffic

00:17:58.540 --> 00:17:59.528
to that, well, great.

00:17:59.528 --> 00:18:01.570
If your service is up,
but not receiving traffic,

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:03.010
that's a bad thing.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:06.272
So really simple--
terminate your service.

00:18:06.272 --> 00:18:07.730
The other thing we
do at this level

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:10.430
is block access to one
critical dependency.

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:14.540
So whatever that is, if it's
you've got a build system

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:18.380
and it needs to load
libraries or other components,

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:18.940
block that.

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:21.590
What happens with
your build system?

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:25.040
If it's a service that has
a persistent data store,

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:27.800
what happens if you block
access to that data store?

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:30.620
Again, it doesn't
have to survive.

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:33.680
If I have a user log in
service and I block access

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:36.352
to that data store and
people can't log in,

00:18:36.352 --> 00:18:37.310
well, that's not great.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:39.357
But at least that's what
I should expect, right?

00:18:39.357 --> 00:18:40.940
And if that's what
I expect and that's

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.530
the way that the
service operates,

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:45.730
well, then that's a good thing.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:46.840
From there, we level up.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:48.970
We block access to
all the dependencies.

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:53.120
And again, we want to ensure
that it acts the way we expect

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:55.153
it to, not that it survives.

00:18:55.153 --> 00:18:56.445
If it survives, that's amazing.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.540
But we want to ensure that
it expects the way we do.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:04.830
So we want to verify that errors
are reported, right, that we

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.870
have proper monitoring on that.

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:09.000
If we block all
those dependencies,

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:11.270
we should at least get
some sort of alert.

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:14.820
And that automated alert
should go to my team.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:16.570
It should have links
to my dashboards.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:20.550
It should have links back to
some sort of documentation

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.570
with a runbook that
says, if this happens,

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:24.570
here's what you look at.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:28.330
Here's what you try
to do to resolve that.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:30.940
The next level up is we
terminate the entire host.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:33.550
So we take down that
node or that VM.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:36.040
And we see if it fails
over to another one.

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:37.330
Hopefully, it does.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:41.110
We verify that that traffic gets
redirected to that new host.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:44.590
Because we don't want to
have to manually do that.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:48.040
This also helps us guarantee
that hosts are replicated

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:52.330
across availability zones and
that similarly, their data

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:54.530
stores are replicated, as well.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:59.720
Level 3 is that we
degrade the environment.

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:02.110
So we're starting to get a
little more complex here.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:04.090
But we do things like
slow down the network.

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:07.600
Start dropping packets,
introduce jitter.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:11.320
We want to verify here
that we can survive this,

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:14.230
or that at least, if
that data is coming in

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:16.840
and it's incomplete because
we've missed packets,

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:19.750
that our systems can
now start to maybe retry

00:20:19.750 --> 00:20:21.550
those, or at least
identify that it's

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:24.590
gotten malformed information.

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.190
We also want to verify that
we have what I call LETS.

00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:30.040
So Google, a while
back, their SRE team

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.170
wrote the Google SRE handbook.

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:33.820
It's published by O'Reilly.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:36.430
And they have this thing
called the four golden signals.

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:39.190
Which-- it's a high,
nice, lofty name.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:40.550
I never remember what it is.

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:44.290
So I reorganized
it and said LETS.

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:47.110
LETS is a nice acronym for
latency, errors, traffic,

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:48.430
and saturation.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:51.190
Latency-- are we seeing
increases in latency?

00:20:51.190 --> 00:20:53.200
Do we have proper
monitoring for that?

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:56.620
Errors-- are we monitoring what
sort of errors we're receiving?

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:58.420
What's the error rate?

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:01.750
Traffic-- are we seeing more
traffic or less traffic?

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:04.810
And we want to monitor
that in both ways.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:08.020
If I'm seeing more traffic,
well, that could be good.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:11.110
It could mean that
whatever we tried,

00:21:11.110 --> 00:21:12.728
users are actually
engaging more,

00:21:12.728 --> 00:21:14.020
so we're seeing higher traffic.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:16.300
Or it could be
bad, that something

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:18.510
that we rolled out,
something that we're testing

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:19.690
is less than optimal.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:24.130
And now we're issuing more
requests against the server.

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:27.100
Similarly, saturation-- do
we have enough resources,

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:31.092
whether that's
computes, I/O, network.

00:21:31.092 --> 00:21:32.550
The great thing
about this, though,

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:34.460
is once you pass this
level, once you're

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:36.450
able to survive a
degraded environment,

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.470
once you're sure that we have
complete monitoring on that,

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.210
that we've got those
metrics, that we've

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:44.720
traced this application,
that we've got proper logs--

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:48.980
this is the time when you can
actually hand off to the SRE.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:50.510
So up until this
point, at Datadog,

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.760
if you write a service,
you write that service,

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:54.140
you're responsible for it.

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:56.750
Which means that
you're on call for it.

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.910
You have to run what you write.

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:00.350
But at this point,
because we know

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:02.450
we have full monitoring
on this, we've

